<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William, Duke of Saxe-Weimar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1598">1598</a>–<a href="/years/1662">1662</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William was Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> from 1620 until his death in 1662. A Protestant commander in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, he was captured at the Battle of Stadtlohn in 1623 and later made peace with the emperor. He headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a>, a literary academy promoting the German language, from 1651. On his death his lands were divided among his sons into Weimar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-saxe-weimar.jpg" alt="William, Duke of Saxe-Weimar" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Richter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William,_Duke_of_Saxe-Weimar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
